{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1240 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e1acc6a76626205ae946b6077355cb2ddfb8825f727dbe6db1acf3c5d73d7dff" :edb/db-before-t 1239 :edb/db-after-t 1240 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534552 50 #inst "2026-10-08T00:46:18.679Z" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157113 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(1,1)$\n\n- $(-4,4)$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157113 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(0,0)$\n2. $(1,1)$\n3. $(-4,4)$" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157113 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{(0, 0)} &= (y + x)^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{(0, 0)} \\\\ = (0 + 0)^{3} \\\\ = 0✓ \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}\\end{vmatrix}_{(1, 1)}|= (y + x)^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{(1, 1)} \\\\ = (1 + 1)^{3} \\\\ = 8 \\\\ \\ne 0\\cdot \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}\\end{vmatrix}_{(-4, 4)}|= (y + x)^{3} \\mid _{(-4, 4)} \\\\\n&= (4 + (-4))^{3} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157113 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0+0)^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,4)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(-4,4)} \\\\  & =(4+(-4))^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157117 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P$." 13194139534552 false] [17592186157117 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P.$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157117 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y - e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y) - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\ninto the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4(4y - e^{3x}) - 3e^{3x} \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 16y - 7e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{(0, 1)} &= (16y - 7e^{3x}) \\mid _{(0, 1)} \\\\\n&= 16(1) - 7e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157117 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y-{e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(4y-{e}^{3x})-3{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =16y-7{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(16y-7{e}^{3x})|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =16(1)-7{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157118 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157118 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157118 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0)$, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{(2, 0)} &= (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\mid _{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} - 4 - (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(4) - \\frac{d}{dx}(y) \\\\\n&= 2x - \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= 2x - (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{(2, 0)} &= (-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y) \\mid _{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= - (2)^{2} + 2(2) + 4 + (0) \\\\\n&= 4 \\\\\n&> 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0$,\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$.\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0)$. So, according to the second derivative test, since\n$\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} \\right|_{(2,0)} > 0$,\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157118 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-4-y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4-(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2x-({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(2,0)} & ={(-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+2(2)+4+(0) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}  \\right|_{(2,0)}  > 0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294947 13194139534552 true] [17592186157297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294964 13194139534552 true] [17592186157297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294971 13194139534552 true] [17592186157297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294978 13194139534552 true] [17592186157301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294961 13194139534552 true] [17592186157301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294985 13194139534552 true] [17592186157301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294992 13194139534552 true] [17592186157301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294999 13194139534552 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294954 13194139534552 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295006 13194139534552 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295013 13194139534552 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295020 13194139534552 true] [17592186157916 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186157916 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $y^{′} - y = x^{3} + 3x^{2}$. Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(-1,-2)$\n2. $(1, 1)$\n3. $(1,-2)$" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157916 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157916 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(-1,-2)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,-2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157917 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534552 true] [17592186157917 1100 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2y + x^{2}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}$ at $P$." 13194139534552 false] [17592186157917 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157917 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+2{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,-1)} & ={(4y+2{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =4(-1)+2(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534552 true] [17592186157929 1100 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = ye^{x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $(0, e)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(0, e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(0, e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(0, e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534552 false] [17592186157929 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186157929 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294947 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294948 13194139534552 true] [17592186294947 1089 "q-163812" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294947 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186294947 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294947 1113 #uuid "6089ab97-a271-5649-93dc-4070bae89004" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294947 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294949 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294950 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294951 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294952 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294953 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294949 13194139534552 true] [17592186294948 1110 #uuid "be3b760c-3192-5da6-a07f-6744370dba67" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294949 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294949 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294949 1111 #uuid "424858ef-f37a-5808-83a0-9890b98fcc15" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294950 1034 "I only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294950 1111 #uuid "494f5b83-f79c-5c64-8d44-cb4acd239e6c" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294951 1034 "III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294951 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294951 1111 #uuid "c51243e6-307a-5d0f-a9c4-8bae47e147c0" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294952 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294952 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294952 1111 #uuid "60935ccf-d0bc-5fe1-a025-b66601ae718c" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294953 1034 "II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294953 1111 #uuid "5ff4395e-0335-55ec-be9a-e6cd945a84e1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294954 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294955 13194139534552 true] [17592186294954 1089 "q-164138" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294954 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534552 true] [17592186294954 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294954 1113 #uuid "a0ab17cf-90f2-5901-a4a1-5dce6cfcb168" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294954 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294956 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294957 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294958 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294959 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294960 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294959 13194139534552 true] [17592186294955 1110 #uuid "f7005e37-c07b-5853-aa37-7ba42e2c2fb3" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294956 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294956 1111 #uuid "cb03cc2c-9e09-547d-b74f-c39558b02352" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294957 1034 "I only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294957 1111 #uuid "17368231-e783-5570-aa08-5bdbf30e2be7" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294958 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294958 1111 #uuid "7763fcc3-fbbf-50a9-8614-a5de03c7d06b" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294959 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294959 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294959 1111 #uuid "a1e54bc3-54c1-5195-97c7-60716c5b6a3e" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294960 1034 "II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294960 1111 #uuid "f26d01e5-939c-591e-896f-2ffc3a8e048f" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294961 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294962 13194139534552 true] [17592186294961 1089 "q-352601" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294961 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186294961 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate ${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}.$\n\n${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294961 1113 #uuid "d17faf10-2df9-589e-89d9-b965765aefa3" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294961 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =(2{y}^{2})^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $(2{y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} & =(8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x})\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{3}+4(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294963 13194139534552 true] [17592186294962 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534552 true] [17592186294962 1041 "field-1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294962 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294963 13194139534552 true] [17592186294962 1110 #uuid "61dcfd99-bd51-5e8e-ab25-09118a55d37f" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294963 1034 "-7" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294963 1111 #uuid "c98340b0-2c80-52f4-8b52-15cc2a7bed10" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294964 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294965 13194139534552 true] [17592186294964 1089 "q-89866" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294964 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534552 true] [17592186294964 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y-4x}{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,5)$\n- $(-2,-4)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294964 1113 #uuid "d14c25af-8d6d-5a92-8882-9d3442744bdf" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294964 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,5)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(1,5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(5)-4(1)}{1} \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-4)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-2,-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-4)-4(-2)}{(-2)} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-3)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-1,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-3)-4(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294966 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294967 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294968 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294969 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294970 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294970 13194139534552 true] [17592186294965 1110 #uuid "374d61dd-775b-5dbd-8032-747a774bb4e3" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294966 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294966 1111 #uuid "5769b439-c2fd-594e-be13-45964ef4721f" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294967 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294967 1111 #uuid "c3ebf348-030c-5fb9-a0cf-d2afa6c62234" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294968 1034 "I only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294968 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294968 1111 #uuid "9653a09c-ca79-54c5-8ef0-ded05dc44a3a" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294969 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294969 1111 #uuid "a18d7219-88ee-5484-bb07-e06ec56d03f8" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294970 1034 "II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294970 1111 #uuid "45006e84-868f-5763-a423-7ab267be2807" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294971 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294972 13194139534552 true] [17592186294971 1089 "q-89869" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294971 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186294971 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294971 1113 #uuid "abdb69a3-efbc-5b10-a499-480d3008bd55" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294971 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294973 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294974 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294975 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294976 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294977 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294976 13194139534552 true] [17592186294972 1110 #uuid "ede1fd09-aa5b-5b2e-b309-1204551b67d4" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294973 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294973 1111 #uuid "76e28705-8b20-5ae5-94dc-898b9206bb76" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294974 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294974 1111 #uuid "70b9c44d-0ba5-5f11-a1d9-dd3fd54a0d1b" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294975 1034 "I only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294975 1111 #uuid "927d8988-a753-5489-9f9e-e2de688f3338" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294976 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294976 1111 #uuid "47be7ca8-115b-55b8-bef8-75e79d48472d" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294977 1034 "II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294977 1111 #uuid "d9ce2f34-520d-559b-a530-4fe705f77f33" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294978 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294979 13194139534552 true] [17592186294978 1089 "q-89871" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294978 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186294978 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(e,1)$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294978 1113 #uuid "602325df-4815-541b-a3da-c6bd15c965f6" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294978 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294980 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294981 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294982 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294983 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294984 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294983 13194139534552 true] [17592186294979 1110 #uuid "7c690bf6-5c44-5c59-b981-48b1409ff4c4" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294980 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294980 1111 #uuid "e0459cd9-b0c9-5a2b-a3fe-0c34023ea426" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294981 1034 "I only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294981 1111 #uuid "7e58f12c-a8c5-5b91-af02-8deb2a00b47d" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294982 1034 "III only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294982 1111 #uuid "1ef4eb47-96ad-5ba3-bbbd-2525c79b68cf" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294983 1034 "None of the points are the stationary points of the function" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294983 1111 #uuid "2c4f3f21-2531-5da4-875a-f2b7caf92a0e" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294984 1034 "II only" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534552 true] [17592186294984 1111 #uuid "5b5b8333-baf9-5528-afbc-3fcb372ef6fa" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294985 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294986 13194139534552 true] [17592186294985 1089 "q-89879" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534552 true] [17592186294985 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294985 1113 #uuid "f555eba5-d8ed-5c9b-af82-7f02d6c04aab" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294985 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,2)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(0,2)} \\\\  & =2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294987 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294988 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294989 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294990 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294991 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294991 13194139534552 true] [17592186294986 1110 #uuid "ea3eef45-2156-5c18-ba47-fe189467b6c0" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294987 1034 "2" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294987 1111 #uuid "df2e10d0-2bbc-5bd2-a690-6e9f506628fb" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294988 1034 "1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294988 1111 #uuid "779a4d2b-f73e-5068-ad16-7cff64866df8" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294989 1034 "0" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294989 1111 #uuid "7a731647-7208-57b0-be91-2270bc83435b" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294990 1034 "-1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294990 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294990 1111 #uuid "b744c5f0-5bc2-59aa-9e90-982eda1bd145" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294991 1034 "-2" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294991 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294991 1111 #uuid "697ac1ab-71bd-56eb-b4d7-140c97f7e720" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294992 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294993 13194139534552 true] [17592186294992 1089 "q-89880" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186294992 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ Given that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)}?$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294992 1113 #uuid "20f93830-129f-5d70-8bb2-53f420e30f93" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294992 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{(y)^{′}{x}^{2}-y({x}^{2})^{′}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}-2xy)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)} & ={\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-2(1))}{(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294994 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294995 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294996 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294997 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294998 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294998 13194139534552 true] [17592186294993 1110 #uuid "b30a6b97-037d-55a9-bc98-e278d57d9161" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294994 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294994 1111 #uuid "b72b13f1-ae70-5825-9154-5a1314671bc7" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294995 1034 "2" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294995 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294995 1111 #uuid "3702ba5b-f03c-57e2-8985-181ba039a003" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294996 1034 "1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294996 1111 #uuid "444fbe07-d59a-5e73-8271-aeef74c06f6f" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294997 1034 "-1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294997 1111 #uuid "9a175538-7a2f-5a79-9477-8a094acd33ab" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294998 1034 "-2" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294998 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186294998 1111 #uuid "5ec9f59c-e37b-51ed-ad90-eb1e32a3e65f" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294999 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295000 13194139534552 true] [17592186294999 1089 "q-89882" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294999 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534552 true] [17592186294999 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294999 1113 #uuid "e9cd8765-dba6-5a71-a78e-2242d11f3089" 13194139534552 true] [17592186294999 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\  & =-1+0+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295001 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295002 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295003 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295004 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295005 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295003 13194139534552 true] [17592186295000 1110 #uuid "327f5619-34f4-5b7d-9d6f-90693b679e05" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295001 1034 "2" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186295001 1111 #uuid "e0694d60-c10c-5a95-89f2-73867d4ad4fc" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295002 1034 "1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186295002 1111 #uuid "5e0e9e5a-40b4-5373-835f-093b29c6a08d" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295003 1034 "0" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186295003 1111 #uuid "5df168b7-d144-5043-a49f-ef81b00c4762" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295004 1034 "-1" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186295004 1111 #uuid "c4349c94-72d4-5c82-88a8-07d88bc5d54c" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295005 1034 "-2" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534552 true] [17592186295005 1111 #uuid "2a11a1ea-cc40-5c2e-b53c-8670bec64f6f" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295007 13194139534552 true] [17592186295006 1089 "q-89887" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534552 true] [17592186295006 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295006 1113 #uuid "e2017d38-ca7e-5dff-a660-bcc0c1d7b463" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295006 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295008 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295009 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295010 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295011 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295012 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1041 "selection" 13194139534552 true] [17592186295007 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295012 13194139534552 true] [175921